<section id="top">
        <div class="page-shell about-page-intro">
            <span class="eyebrow">Quickler in Canada</span>
            <h1>WhatsApp field compliance software in Canada.</h1>
            <p class="page-lede">Canadian field firms already coordinate crews over WhatsApp. Quickler turns that same channel into inspections, checklists and certificates. A worker on a site in Toronto, Calgary or Halifax answers questions by text, voice note or photo, and every answer becomes a clean, structured digital record on a manager dashboard, with red, amber and green fault status. When a formal document is needed, a PDF exports straight from that record. No app to install, no per-user fee, no hardware to ship across the provinces.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight">
        <div class="content-duo">
            <div class="section-frame">
                <div class="section-header">
                    <div>
                        <p class="section-label">The output</p>
                        <h2>A structured digital record, not just a piece of paper.</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="detail-body">
                    <p>Most field tools leave a manager with a stack of PDFs to read one by one. Quickler leads with something more useful: a live, machine-readable record of every job, sortable and searchable on one dashboard.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Every inspection lands on the dashboard with red, amber and green fault status</li>
                        <li>Managers see what failed, where, and who recorded it without opening a file</li>
                        <li>Photos and voice notes attach to the exact question they answer</li>
                        <li>A PDF exports from the record whenever a client, insurer or regulator wants a document</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>For a Canadian firm running checks across multiple sites and time zones, that shift from a pile of documents to one structured record is the practical difference.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="section-frame">
                <div class="section-header">
                    <div>
                        <p class="section-label">No app, no friction</p>
                        <h2>Crews use the WhatsApp account already on their phone.</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="detail-body">
                    <p>Field work in Canada often means subcontractors, seasonal crews and bilingual teams who change from job to job. Asking each of them to download, log in to and learn a new app is where adoption stalls.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The field worker opens WhatsApp, which they already use every day</li>
                        <li>There is nothing to install, no account to create, no password to reset on site</li>
                        <li>It works the same on any Canadian carrier and on any phone</li>
                        <li>Because there is no per-user fee, adding a casual crew member costs nothing</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>The barrier to getting a worker recording proper compliance data drops to one message.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

